   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-776371",
   "speaker": "Anthony Albanese",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-10-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-776372",
   "speaker": "Greg Hunt",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-10-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-776605",
   "speaker": "Santo Santoro",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-06",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The bills were introduced together and passed through first reading without formalities, with a motion to have three of them listed separately on the Notice Paper."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-776606",
   "speaker": "Santo Santoro",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-06",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The government introduced three bills: the Copyright Amendment Bill 2006 legalises time-shifting of broadcasts and format-shifting of music, newspapers and books for private use, introduces new exceptions for parody and satire, strengthens technological protection measures, and creates new criminal offences targeting piracy and unauthorised pay TV access; the Environment and Heritage Legislation Amendment Bill (No. 1) 2006 amends the EPBC Act to streamline environmental assessments, cut red tape, strengthen compliance and enforcement, and transition the Register of the National Estate to a non-statutory archive after five years; and the Inspector of Transport Security Bill 2006 establishes an independent Inspector to conduct no-blame inquiries into major transport security incidents, with powers to compel evidence and protect confidentiality."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777128",
   "speaker": "Kim Carr",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-29",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The speaker argued that the Environment and Heritage Legislation Amendment Bill (No. 1) 2006 should be rejected, announcing the opposition would vote against it and moving a second reading amendment requiring climate change to be factored into environmental decisions and a climate change trigger in the EPBC Act. The speech criticised the 409-page bill for being rushed without proper consultation, weakening ministerial accountability and public participation, removing recovery plans for threatened species, eliminating third-party appeal rights, and failing to address climate change—citing the Howard Government's $60 million subsidy to Chevron and the loss of 500 threatened ecological communities from the listing backlog."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777229",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Carr moved an amendment to the motion that the bill be read a second time, adding additional Senate conditions. The debate resumed from 29 November on Senator Santoro's original motion."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777230",
   "speaker": "Bob Brown",
   "party": "Greens",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-11-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The speaker argued that the Environment and Heritage Legislation Amendment Bill (No. 1) 2006 would weaken Australia's primary environmental law by removing mandatory recovery plans for threatened species, eliminating third-party appeal rights, and giving the minister greater discretion to politicise scientific assessments. The speaker also criticised the government for failing to include a climate change trigger in the bill and for not consulting conservation groups or state governments during its development."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777266",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-01",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777267",
   "speaker": "Kim Carr",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-01",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The minister argued against the opposition's climate change trigger amendments, stating they would be a blunt regulatory tool duplicating existing greenhouse gas measurement systems and could harm the coal industry, while defending the government's $2 billion investment in climate measures and support for clean coal, carbon capture, and nuclear energy."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777468",
   "speaker": "Steve Hutchins",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-05",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The minister opposed opposition amendments to introduce a climate change trigger in the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act, arguing it would impose unnecessary regulatory burdens on coal and gas projects and that the government's existing policies are more practical. He also criticised Labor's inconsistency on coal and nuclear energy."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777788",
   "speaker": "Alan Ferguson",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2006-12-07",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Siewert moved amendments to insert national environmental significance triggers for greenhouse gas emissions (100,000 tonnes of CO2 per annum), land clearing, unsustainable water use, and large dams, arguing these are needed to address biodiversity loss, water over-allocation, and climate change, citing the State of the Environment report and specific threatened species."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-777789",
   "speaker": "Ian Campbell",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
